{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yang/correspondence_text.clj | sed -n '1,58p'; nl -ba collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md | sed -n '200,237p'; cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794790000000-engineer-d1-r14-companion-subset-retention.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yang.correspondence-text\n     2\t  \"JVM text capture pilot. Acceptance still requires a pinned dependency closure\n     3\t   and per-compilation replay of every claimed artifact.\"\n     4\t  (:require [clojure.edn :as edn]))\n     5\t\n     6\t\n     7\t(def profile\n     8\t  {:yang.correspondence.text/schema 1\n     9\t   :host :jvm\n    10\t   :clojure-version (clojure-version)\n    11\t   :java-version (System/getProperty \"java.version\")\n    12\t   :java-vendor (System/getProperty \"java.vendor\")\n    13\t   :encoding :utf-8\n    14\t   :normalization :none\n    15\t   :printer 'clojure.core/pr-str\n    16\t   :printer-options {:meta true :length nil :level nil :readably true\n    17\t                     :dup false :namespace-maps false}\n    18\t   :reader 'clojure.edn/read\n    19\t   :reader-options {:values 1 :eof-required true :ambient-readers false\n    20\t                    :tags :unsupported}\n    21\t   :domain :edn-with-readable-metadata\n    22\t   :fidelity-rule :fixed-jvm-integer-wrappers-v1})\n    23\t\n    24\t\n    25\t(defn- kind\n    26\t  [x]\n    27\t  (cond (map? x) :map (vector? x) :vector (set? x) :set\n    28\t        (seq? x) :list (sequential? x) :sequence :else :scalar))\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\t(def ^:private fixed-integer-classes #{Byte Short Integer Long})\n    32\t\n    33\t\n    34\t(def ^:private supported-numeric-classes\n    35\t  #{Byte Short Integer Long clojure.lang.BigInt java.math.BigInteger\n    36\t    java.math.BigDecimal clojure.lang.Ratio Double Float})\n    37\t\n    38\t\n    39\t(defn- numeric-faithful?\n    40\t  [a b]\n    41\t  (and (number? a) (number? b)\n    42\t       (let [ca (class a) cb (class b)]\n    43\t         (cond\n    44\t           (and (contains? fixed-integer-classes ca)\n    45\t                (contains? fixed-integer-classes cb)) (= (long a) (long b))\n    46\t           (and (= ca cb) (contains? supported-numeric-classes ca)) (.equals a b)\n    47\t           :else false))))\n    48\t\n    49\t\n    50\t(defn- mismatch\n    51\t  [a b path]\n    52\t  (cond\n    53\t    (not= (kind a) (kind b)) {:path path :reason :kind :actual (kind a) :read (kind b)}\n    54\t    (not= (some? (meta a)) (some? (meta b))) {:path path :reason :metadata-presence}\n    55\t    :else\n    56\t    (or (when (some? (meta a)) (mismatch (meta a) (meta b) (conj path :metadata)))\n    57\t        (case (kind a)\n    58\t          :map (if (not= (count a) (count b))\n   200\tThe D1 key set is closed; vectors preserve declared order and contain no duplicate declarations. Empty artifact/provenance vectors are allowed for a tree-only consumer, whose claimed coverage must say tree-only. `artifacts` lists lowered images only; canonical roots are retrievable row closures listed separately. Native image identity need not equal its storage address: the publisher already distinguishes checksums and stored images (`publish.cljc:159-185,204-208`). The record's address is the ordinary Jing content address of the whole map; no self-address field.\n   201\t\n   202\t`evidence` resolves to **OracleEvidence verbatim**: same EDN data shape, schema/version, field names, vectors, site ids, source-hash value and classifications. Do not project a subset or copy its rows into a second durable site-table shape. Store the whole value through a lossless codec and prove round-trip equality; preserve the original checked-in EDN bytes separately as a source artifact when byte-level archival is needed. Jing storage addressing does not replace the oracle's own canonical-source-hash algorithm. New adoption evidence must use the oracle format as frozen; unsupported representational needs require an explicit versioned extension owned by that format, never an ad hoc competing table.\n   203\t\n   204\t`inputs` names the retrievable source/adaptation/fixture/profile inputs required by the recipe, including the inlined adapter and declarations that the oracle actually hashed. A repository commit string alone is not a fetchable reconstruction input. `roots` names the produced code trees. Expansion records and admitted source-envelope identities required by the bindings must be in the retrievable input closure. The input/source artifacts may be data blobs; they must not be represented as UAST addresses unless they actually are UAST.\n   205\t\n   206\tThe recipe is a closed versioned data description, proposed as:\n   207\t\n   208\t```clojure\n   209\t{:yang.correspondence.recipe/schema 1\n   210\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/function <qualified interpreter identifier>\n   211\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/profile <address of exact reconstruction profile>\n   212\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/input-roles [[<role keyword> <input index>] ...]\n   213\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/evidence-contract <oracle's exact schema identity>\n   214\t :yang.correspondence.recipe/coverage <address of declared coverage policy>}\n   215\t```\n   216\t\n   217\tThe recipe profile pins frontend/compiler revision, grammar/options, expansion environment and recorded origins/attempts, all code-producing transformations, source canonicalization, site-table derivation, and lowering/provenance rules. The consumer must implement that exact recipe/profile. A missing upstream canonicalization rule is not repaired by choosing a plausible hash. The exact oracle schema identity remains the dependency in §1.1.\n   218\t\n   219\t`origin-bindings` is an added relation binding evidence-local sites to actual durable occurrences; **it is not a replacement attribution table**. It preserves the oracle's ephemeral ids literally. Validate every endpoint, reconstructed tree and path. A site's mapping must be unique within the claimed compilation. Multiple replay records can name the same durable occurrence only where they explicitly replay the same admission; distinct admissions must remain distinct. An oracle run without durable expansion/source records cannot be promoted merely by changing `:ephemeral` to `:expansion`. It may remain useful oracle evidence without a durable binding. To accept a relying consumer, reproduce the actual compilation under recorded durable admissions/expansions and verify its correspondence; do not retroactively assert an unrecorded event.\n   220\t\n   221\t### 4.2 Independent reconstruction and discovery\n   222\t\n   223\tThe D1 producer is the compilation/publication composition that can retain inputs, origins and outputs. The canonical compiler and lowerer remain oblivious to attribution policy except for the side artifacts they emit. D1 is an ordinary record on a separate publication/ledger stream, discoverable by its address and its code/evidence associations. The Python site-set record is a concrete precedent for “record names manifest, manifest keeps schema” (`siteset.cljc:5-22,79-101`). The implementation must pin the actual discovery schema, signature/index projection and retention owner before acceptance; this document does not pretend the current linker automatically fetches D1.\n   224\t\n   225\tVerification proceeds in this order:\n   226\t\n   227\t1. Fetch D1, recipe, OracleEvidence and every required source/row/provenance artifact; verify their addresses and structural schemas. Reject an unsupported oracle schema before interpreting any row.\n   228\t2. Verify each code image under its own execution contract and its existing derivation chain. Report content integrity separately from code derivation.\n   229\t3. Reconstruct the source/adaptation compilation under the exact recipe, using recorded source admissions and expansion attempts; recompute final roots and eligible occurrence sets. Recompute the oracle's canonical-source-hash using its exact rule.\n   230\t4. Independently re-derive the oracle site declarations from compiled AST argument expressions. Compare with OracleEvidence verbatim under that schema's comparison rules. Do not make the producer's serialized classifications the verifier's input oracle.\n   231\t5. Reconstruct every claimed ephemeral-to-persisted binding, C1 marker set and instruction mapping. Verify exact coverage and all multiplicities, including helpers that receive no upstream credit.\n   232\t6. Execute the attribution query over separately scoped evidence/occurrence/row relations; require actual body entry in both required oracle C lanes. Reconstruct the credit set and report withdrawn witnesses and stale declarations. R7 requires re-derivation, not just re-running a query over trusted declarations (`ruling-of-record.md:48-50`).\n   233\t7. Publish a separate verification result naming D1's address, recipe/profile, verifier revision, inputs, outcomes and evidence. D1 cannot mark itself “verified”. The first additional consumer's acceptance must cite this completed result.\n   234\t\n   235\tAn implementation may share the schema decoder and canonical primitives, but a verifier must not call the producer's table derivation as its only “independent” check. The existing table generator resolves the oracle's own private derivation vars (`latte_oracle_tables.clj:15-18`); regeneration through it alone does not satisfy R7.\n   236\t\n   237\tD1 does not revise old site tables, force old module manifests to schema 2, or automatically validate the oracle's historical evidence. If actual integration requires changing a closed publication record, that specific schema revision returns to the A2 inventory. Unknown recipes refuse verification; `:trusted` is never substituted for `:verifying` (linker design `:1902-1921`).\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-17 09:39:07 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-17 04:39:07 EST\n\nThe requested publication is **incomplete**. I recorded the worktree findings in [the r14 findings file](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794790000000-engineer-d1-r14-companion-subset-retention.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md).\n\nThe subset extraction was interrupted after producing a partial store; I removed that incomplete artifact. The companion and verification result remain blocked by missing recipe, provenance, image-verification, and complete two-lane trace evidence. The full-store retention owner remains pending designation.\n\nThe standing battery passed **58 tests and 708 assertions**. Kondo reported **0 errors and 1 warning** across the inherited battery. The frozen v1 file’s SHA-256 remains unchanged. No commit was made.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
